      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-bacc7d0e399e::sentence-3",
          "documentId": "doc-bacc7d0e399e",
          "text": "Turning the Tables, Russia Sends Virus Aid to U.S.\n",
          "start": 27,
          "end": 78,
          "context": "Supported by\n Turning the Tables, Russia Sends Virus Aid to U.S.\n A planeload of coronavirus aid to New York was a propaganda coup for the Kremlin — tempered by an intensifying epidemic at home.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-ff8f3576e3da::sentence-16",
          "documentId": "doc-ff8f3576e3da",
          "text": "The US sent $5.6 million in medical supplies to Russia to help it combat the coronavirus pandemic. ",
          "start": 2405,
          "end": 2504,
          "context": "Democrats were also outraged over the difference in the cost and amount of medical aid provided by Russia to the US versus that delivered by the US to Russia.\n The US sent $5.6 million in medical supplies to Russia to help it combat the coronavirus pandemic.  A video posted to the Twitter account of the US Embassy in Moscow in June showed an American military cargo plane arriving with some of the aid, including 200 ventilators.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-ff8f3576e3da::sentence-15",
          "documentId": "doc-ff8f3576e3da",
          "text": "Democrats were also outraged over the difference in the cost and amount of medical aid provided by Russia to the US versus that delivered by the US to Russia.\n",
          "start": 2246,
          "end": 2405,
          "context": "(Russia first said it would charge the US $660,000 for the machines and other supplies — roughly half the cost of the shipment — but later a government official told BuzzFeed News that it had not billed Washington because the countries had agreed to swap medical aid).  Democrats were also outraged over the difference in the cost and amount of medical aid provided by Russia to the US versus that delivered by the US to Russia.\n The US sent $5.6 million in medical supplies to Russia to help it combat the coronavirus pandemic. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-ff8f3576e3da::sentence-19",
          "documentId": "doc-ff8f3576e3da",
          "text": "Speaking alongside members of his coronavirus task force in the White House Rose Garden on March 30, Trump said, “Russia sent us a very, very large planeload of things, medical equipment, which was very nice.”\n",
          "start": 2848,
          "end": 3058,
          "context": "The Kremlin, the US State Department, and Trump himself hailed the delivery as a rare example of collaboration between the two adversarial nations to fight a common enemy.\n Speaking alongside members of his coronavirus task force in the White House Rose Garden on March 30, Trump said, “Russia sent us a very, very large planeload of things, medical equipment, which was very nice.”\n The US and Russia remain among the countries with the highest number of cases of COVID-19, and both are currently experiencing new waves of infections. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-bacc7d0e399e::sentence-63",
          "documentId": "doc-bacc7d0e399e",
          "text": "The government-backed Russian Direct Investment Fund, which paid for half the shipment, said it was working with U.S. companies to deliver medical supplies to Russia if they are needed.\n",
          "start": 8919,
          "end": 9105,
          "context": "Russia, however, insisted the aid flight to New York was part of a longer-term strategy to fight the pandemic on a global level.  The government-backed Russian Direct Investment Fund, which paid for half the shipment, said it was working with U.S. companies to deliver medical supplies to Russia if they are needed.\n On Thursday, the latest target of Russia’s virus diplomacy emerged: Serbia. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-bacc7d0e399e::sentence-10",
          "documentId": "doc-bacc7d0e399e",
          "text": "After plans for the shipment stirred criticism on both sides of the Atlantic, the Foreign Ministry in Moscow said on Thursday that the two countries had in fact evenly split the cost of the medical goods and that Russia could depend on future aid from the United States in fighting the coronavirus.\n",
          "start": 1152,
          "end": 1451,
          "context": "But with the pandemic increasingly bearing down on Russia, the Kremlin’s propaganda machine appeared to avoid trumpeting the aid shipment lest Russians think that the government was ignoring their own plight.\n After plans for the shipment stirred criticism on both sides of the Atlantic, the Foreign Ministry in Moscow said on Thursday that the two countries had in fact evenly split the cost of the medical goods and that Russia could depend on future aid from the United States in fighting the coronavirus.\n “We are certain that if it’s necessary they will in the future be able to help Russia,” the Russian Foreign Ministry spokeswoman, Maria Zakharova, said in a statement.\n"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
