          "context": "Democrats were also outraged over the difference in the cost and amount of medical aid provided by Russia to the US versus that delivered by the US to Russia.\n The US sent $5.6 million in medical supplies to Russia to help it combat the coronavirus pandemic.  A video posted to the Twitter account of the US Embassy in Moscow in June showed an American military cargo plane arriving with some of the aid, including 200 ventilators.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-bacc7d0e399e::sentence-32",
          "documentId": "doc-bacc7d0e399e",
          "text": "On Aug. 23, the Food and Drug Administration granted full approval to Pfizer-BioNTech’s coronavirus vaccine for people 16 and up, paving the way for an increase in mandates in both the public and private sectors. ",
          "start": 4864,
          "end": 5077,
          "context": "- Vaccine rules.  On Aug. 23, the Food and Drug Administration granted full approval to Pfizer-BioNTech’s coronavirus vaccine for people 16 and up, paving the way for an increase in mandates in both the public and private sectors.  Private companies have been increasingly mandating vaccines for employees. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-bacc7d0e399e::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-bacc7d0e399e",
          "text": "A planeload of coronavirus aid to New York was a propaganda coup for the Kremlin — tempered by an intensifying epidemic at home.\n",
          "start": 78,
          "end": 207,
          "context": "Turning the Tables, Russia Sends Virus Aid to U.S.\n A planeload of coronavirus aid to New York was a propaganda coup for the Kremlin — tempered by an intensifying epidemic at home.\n MOSCOW — In the early 1990s, amid the poverty-ridden collapse of the Soviet Union, American food aid in the form of a flood of cheap chicken thighs — Russians called them “Bush legs” — symbolized the humiliating downfall of a superpower.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-bacc7d0e399e::sentence-54",
          "documentId": "doc-bacc7d0e399e",
          "text": "President Biden announced that all civilian federal employees would have to be vaccinated against the coronavirus or submit to regular testing, social distancing, mask requirements and restrictions on most travel.\n",
          "start": 7173,
          "end": 7387,
          "context": "The Pentagon announced that it would seek to make coronavirus vaccinations mandatory for the country’s 1.3 million active-duty troops “no later” than the middle of September.  President Biden announced that all civilian federal employees would have to be vaccinated against the coronavirus or submit to regular testing, social distancing, mask requirements and restrictions on most travel.\n The Russian military said last week that it had also dispatched at least 15 planeloads of medical equipment and personnel to help Italy fight the virus. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-ff8f3576e3da::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-ff8f3576e3da",
          "text": "As the US was bracing for a major wave of seriously ill coronavirus patients in March, President Donald Trump asked President Vladimir Putin in a phone call for help. ",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 167,
          "context": "As the US was bracing for a major wave of seriously ill coronavirus patients in March, President Donald Trump asked President Vladimir Putin in a phone call for help.  In response, Moscow sent 45 ventilators and other medical supplies in crates stamped “From Russia, With Love.”\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-ff8f3576e3da::sentence-19",
          "documentId": "doc-ff8f3576e3da",
          "text": "Speaking alongside members of his coronavirus task force in the White House Rose Garden on March 30, Trump said, “Russia sent us a very, very large planeload of things, medical equipment, which was very nice.”\n",
          "start": 2848,
